079_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-037_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-119_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-063_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-005_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-087_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-057_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-019_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-105_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-029_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-067_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-053_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-083_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\ninput/data/sub-035_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\n# Validate the claim and its presentation\n\nSelect checks that could expose a consequential mistake; avoid tests that merely restate the implementation.\nReuse established scientific libraries and validators, while keeping the expected result independent of the code under test.\n\n## Scientific checks\n\n- Confirm source meanings and units, selected population, joins, duplicate handling, denominators and missingness.\n  Distinguish independent exclusion flags from their unique-row union.\n- Check known examples and relevant boundaries: missing-token forms, invalid/nonfinite values, empty groups, unit conversions, unequal counts or weights.\n  Distinguish absent measurements or coverage from genuine zero values; justify the resulting analysis population or spatial domain.\n  The task contract determines which cases matter.\n- Match uncertainty and resampling to the independent sampling unit.\n  Compare paired observations only for a paired design; distinguish descriptive change from a causal effect.\n- Use independent summaries, a trusted reference implementation or justified controls; prespecify tolerances.\n  When custom numerical code determines inference, check its full statistic against an independent formula or trusted implementation, including quantities that change under resampling.\n  Sensitivity analyses test assumptions, not select favorable results.\n  Keep actual-data correctness separate from the reusable input contract.\n\n## Useful figures\n\nChoose a compact figure when it clarifies the question; a small table can suffice for a simple result.\nShow the relevant individual variation or distribution and uncertainty, rather than only a summary when that hides a material limitation.\nInclude all predeclared sensitivity variants, with context clearly labeled.\n\nGenerate from checked data using established plotting tools.\nRetain editable source, generation command and a rendered preview.\nInspect at intended display size: units, labels, legends, clipping, series identity and interval meaning must agree with the checked table.\nParsing SVG or checking file existence is not visual review.\nIf rendering was unavailable, report that limit rather than crediting a later reviewer to the original worker.\n\n## Fresh replay when warranted\n\nChoose replay when it resolves a material execution uncertainty; do not require it for every delivery or documentary edit.\nIn a disposable copy of the exact delivered state, obtain exact inputs and restore the recorded environment.\nRemove only declared generated outputs, without deleting source data or shared annex content; keep expected artifacts elsewhere.\nRun the documented entry point and require at least one regenerated output.\nA successful no-op must fail this check.\n\nCompare deterministic artifacts by digest or declared semantic/numeric tolerances. Table identifiers, schema and labels need exact agreement; handle missing/nonfinite values explicitly before numeric comparisons.\nPixel differences may reflect rendering rather than changed data; inspect the actual figure separately.\n\nRecord the environment, platform, caches and external services used.\nA fresh checkout is not a different host; rebuilding the scientific result is not automatically rebuilding every report or validating native provenance discovery.\nRetrieve original outputs and requested logs separately when testing delivery; metadata availability does not prove payload retrieval.\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\nDoes increasing potential monetary gain produce a greater fMRI response in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) in the NARPS equal-indifference group?\n\nTest the hypothesis that this response increases with potential gain.\nParticipants performed a mixed-gambles task, deciding whether to accept gambles with possible gains and losses.\nIn this group, the range of potential gains was twice the range of potential losses.\n\nInvestigate the question at the group-analysis stage using the supplied 54 participant gain-effect maps and metadata.\nPreprocessing and individual modeling are fixed inputs, described in `upstream-methods.md`; do not repeat them or download time-series scans.\nChoose scientifically defensible group models, quality checks, software and visualizations.\nAccount for what the supplied effect estimates measure and the limits of their upstream model.\nUse whole-brain correction for multiple comparisons when evaluating support for the hypothesis, as required by the original study; justify how you identify vmPFC.\n\nProduce a concise scientific report with informative figures and the code and results supporting your conclusions.\nExplain uncertainty, exclusions and limitations, including what a lack of support would mean.\nPrior teams' answers are not ground truth or a required result.\n\nSource: [NARPS original analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf).\n{\n  \"study\": \"NARPS Hypothesis 1: group inference from fixed participant effects\",\n  \"collection\": \"https://neurovault.org/collections/4932/\",\n  \"team\": \"AO86\",\n  \"image_metadata_api\": \"https://neurovault.org/api/collections/4932/images/?limit=2000\",\n  \"selection\": \"All single-subject unthresholded hypo1 cope images; participant IDs exactly match the 54 equalIndifference participants in the pinned source table.\",\n  \"maps\": 54,\n  \"map_bytes\": 96387264,\n  \"map_type\": \"participant contrast parameter estimate, not a z/t statistic or a group result\",\n  \"spatial_reference\": \"MNI152NLin2009cAsym\",\n  \"upstream_processing_fixed\": true,\n  \"cohort_source\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenNeuroDatasets/ds001734/64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268/participants.tsv\",\n    \"git_commit\": \"64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268\",\n    \"sha256\": \"d127d700f9a519338da295ea56455bf7bb46ca572f726da72ef7b57c310687b8\",\n    \"transformation\": \"Select group == equalIndifference; preserve all columns and values.\"\n  },\n  \"methods_source\": {